  "worked_solution": "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and  $x=-\\text{i}$ is a root, its conjugate  $x=\\text{i}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-\\text{i})(x+\\text{i})=0\n$$\n\nMultiplying out the parentheses and simplifying gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-(\\text{i})^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-(-1) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has roots $x=\\pm \\text{i},$ but the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ is not $-2.$ Therefore, to find the desired equation, we multiply the equation above by $-2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2({x}^{2}+1) & =0 \\\\ -2{x}^{2}-2 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
